<item>
            <title>[LU-20278] use unwritten extents for batched preallocation in osd-ldiskfs</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20278</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;manipulations with ldiskfs&apos;s orphan list are found to be tricky with number of drawbacks and complications (e.g. a single inode can be represented by few LU objects in a corner case and then discarding preallocated blocks becomes a problem).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Andreas suggested to use fallocate and unwritten extents to get rid of this complexity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I made a prototype which allocates few blocks as an unwritten extent, then call ldiskfs_bread() to convert a block into a regular one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;so here is a  benchmark:&lt;br/&gt;
 256K 32-byte records are appended in 6 threads (real cores), each write is measured in usec &amp;#8211; this was repeated 10 times for every &quot;approach&quot;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;1) good old getblk, no optimizations: 6.3 usec / write&lt;br/&gt;
2) brm : 2.8 usec / write &amp;#8211; 2x improvement&lt;br/&gt;
3) brm + fallocate : 1.5 usec / write &amp;#8211; 4x improvement&lt;br/&gt;
4) brm + current prealloc: 0.8 usec / write &amp;#8211; almost 8x improvement&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;terms:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul class=&quot;alternate&quot; type=&quot;square&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;brm is a 4-slots cache telling whether specific block is allocated or not, if block is allocated, then we can omit per-object semaphore (which is to protect extent tree manipulations).&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;line #4 is for current master: ldiskfs_map_blocks(), orphan list, late i_size and all the problems observed so far.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


&lt;p&gt;the most significant improvement is brought by brm. so probably brm + unwritten extents is good enough for a while (4x improvement)&lt;/p&gt;</description>
